## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a streaming PCA pipeline that handles drift, ensures per-tenant isolation, and meets latency constraints in a real-time system.

## Key Concepts
- IncrementalPCA for streaming data without full batch recomputation
- Rolling window variance tracking to monitor feature distributions
- Per-feature mean imputation for handling missing values
- Online standardization using incremental mean/variance updates
- Dynamic component retention based on cumulative explained variance threshold (95%)
- Loadings drift detection and mapping to actionable alerts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Streaming PCA update per tenant
function updatePCAOnStream(pcaState, x) {
  const { rollingWindow, scaler, pca } = pcaState;

  // 1. Impute missing values with running feature means
  const xImputed = imputeWithRollingMeans(x, rollingWindow.featureMeans);

  // 2. Scale using online StandardScaler
  const xScaled = scaler.transform(xImputed);

  // 3. Incrementally update PCA
  pca.partial_fit(xScaled);

  // 4. Recompute explained variance and retain components (≥95%)
  const nComponents = getComponentCount(pca.explained_variance_ratio_, 0.95);

  // 5. Update rolling window statistics
  rollingWindow.update(xImputed);

  return { pca, nComponents };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you distinguish concept drift from normal random fluctuations in the variance explained by each component?
- What strategies would you use to handle tenants with vastly different feature distributions while sharing a single model architecture?
- How do you balance the trade-off between window size (responsiveness to drift) and stability of the PCA decomposition?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an online, drift-aware PCA system that remains interpretable and compliant under strict latency constraints typical of autonomous vehicle systems.

## Key Concepts
- Online incremental PCA (IPCA) with forgetting factors
- Concept drift detection and adaptation strategies
- Dynamic feature addition/removal via partial-fit methods
- Loading stability tracking for interpretability preservation
- Millisecond-latency optimization through parallel computation
- Governance-aligned explainability through loading-to-alert mappings

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: partial_fit on new data, adjust window, recompute top loadings
function updateIPCA(newData, currentModel, windowSize, forgettingFactor) {
  // Partial fit with forgetting factor
  const updatedModel = partialFit(newData, currentModel, forgettingFactor);
  // Adjust moving window
  const windowedModel = adjustWindow(updatedModel, windowSize);
  // Recpute interpretable loadings
  return computeTopLoadings(windowedModel);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you set drift-detection thresholds to balance automatic fallback versus adaptation?
- What validation approach would you use comparing synthetic drift scenarios against real-world telemetry patterns?
- How do you ensure loading stability while maintaining computational efficiency?

## Why This Is Asked

